{tag:'A STORY ABOUT GITHUB COPILOT SDK',title:'Climbing the<br><em>delegation</em><br>staircase.',desc:'Every step gave us more power. Every step asked something new of us.',scene:'stairs',label:'same ambition. a new step.',annotation:'How far up<br>can we go?',section:'The question',note:'Open with the question, not the SDK. This is a story about what we can delegate — and what we still have to own.'},
{tag:'01 / THE MACHINE · 1940s',title:'We delegated<br><em>arithmetic.</em>',desc:'ENIAC made large numerical calculations practical at a new scale. Humans still had to translate the task into the machine’s configuration.',gain:'Repeated numerical calculation.',cost:'Wiring, configuration and an exact procedure.',scene:'eniac',label:'a calculation, made physical',annotation:'An evening<br>in the archive.',section:'A little history',note:'Keep this short. Ballistic calculations are a useful historical example. The machine executes arithmetic; humans specify the procedure. The photograph is the original image extracted from your copilot-sdk-story-v2-visual.pptx. Source recorded in that deck: https://www.army.mil/article/283485/the_army_at_250_did_you_know_the_army_invented_this'},
{tag:'02 / THE COMPILER · 1957',title:'We stopped writing<br><em>for the machine.</em>',desc:'With FORTRAN, a compiler could translate a higher-level description into machine instructions.',gain:'Translation into machine code.',cost:'Precise algorithms expressed in a formal language.',scene:'compiler',label:'intent → language → instructions',annotation:'One level up.',section:'A little history',note:'The compiler takes over translation, not responsibility for the algorithm. Avoid suggesting natural-language intent is enough.'},
{tag:'03 / THE HIDDEN PART',title:'Abstraction moves<br><em>the problem.</em>',desc:'A simpler surface can hide a more complicated system. The 1968 software crisis gave that growing gap a name.',gain:'Distance from low-level details.',cost:'Understanding the failures beneath the surface.',scene:'iceberg',label:'the interface is only the tip',annotation:'Still there.<br>Just less visible.',section:'The price',note:'Use the iceberg as the recurring warning. Do not claim abstraction alone caused the software crisis.'},
{tag:'04 / APOLLO · A USEFUL ANALOGY',title:'An agent needs<br><em>a spacecraft.</em>',desc:'A capable astronaut still needed instruments, procedures, tools and feedback from mission control.',gain:'Action within a supported mission.',cost:'Designing the environment that makes action reliable.',scene:'apollo',label:'capability + tools + feedback',annotation:'The OG harness :)',section:'The harness',note:'An analogy, not an architectural equivalence. Apollo’s decisions were distributed among the crew, software, procedures and mission control. DSKY and verbs/nouns can connect to your drawing.'},
{tag:'05 / MORE STEPS, SAME PATTERN',title:'Less plumbing.<br><em>New obligations.</em>',desc:'Memory management, objects and remote calls moved work behind interfaces. Each interface came with assumptions.',gain:'Bookkeeping and implementation details.',cost:'Lifetimes, boundaries, latency and partial failure.',scene:'layers',label:'GC / objects / remote calls',annotation:'Abstraction ≠<br>disappearance.',section:'A little history',note:'Treat GC, OOP and RPC as three examples, not a strict chronological ladder. Choose one concrete trade-off for each.'},
{tag:'06 / THE LANGUAGE MODEL',title:'We can describe<br><em>the outcome.</em>',desc:'A model can interpret a request and propose a path. But a plausible answer is not yet completed work.',gain:'Drafting and interpreting less formal instructions.',cost:'Context, ambiguity and checking the result.',scene:'brain',label:'a powerful engine, on its own',annotation:'An answer is not<br>an outcome.',section:'The new step',note:'Do not frame this as guaranteed reasoning. The important distinction is between generating a response and completing a verified task.'},
{tag:'07 / THE AGENT HARNESS',title:'Give the model<br><em>a working loop.</em>',desc:'The harness brings context, tools, permissions and feedback together. The agent can act, inspect what happened and try again.',gain:'Multi-step execution inside an environment.',cost:'Boundaries, evidence and a definition of done.',scene:'harness',label:'observe → act → check → repeat',annotation:'Not just a prompt.<br>A working system.',section:'The new step',note:'Explain what is already inside the harness. Separate the agent loop from the outer business workflow. Click the loop button to illustrate tool use and feedback.'},
{tag:'08 / THE SDK · THE CENTRAL IDEA',title:'Now the harness<br>is <em>callable.</em>',desc:'The agent you direct by hand becomes a component your application can invoke, observe and coordinate.',gain:'Programmatic access to an agent runtime.',cost:'Integration, lifecycle and responsibility for the outcome.',scene:'sdk',label:'your application → SDK → CLI runtime',annotation:'A building block.<br>Not just a chat box.',section:'Architecture',note:'The SDK communicates with the Copilot CLI runtime over JSON-RPC. A C# app is one client. VS Code uses an Agent Host and adapter, not the exact same client path.'},
{tag:'09 / TWO WAYS IN',title:'One capability.<br><em>Different controls.</em>',desc:'Your code can call the SDK. In VS Code, the Agent Host connects the interface to the Copilot harness.',scene:'architecture',label:'click a layer to inspect it',annotation:'UI ≠ SDK<br>≠ orchestration',section:'Architecture',note:'Use the buttons to show the two entry paths. MAF is optional orchestration above a provider; it is not required for the SDK. Sources: github.com/github/copilot-sdk and code.visualstudio.com/blogs/2026/08/26/agent-host-architecture.'},
{tag:'10 / MICROSOFT AGENT FRAMEWORK',title:'Delegate work.<br><em>Keep the gates.</em>',desc:'A small workflow: assign a change, let the agent work, run a check, then accept or return it for repair.',scene:'workflow',label:'an illustrative workflow — not a live agent',annotation:'Evidence before<br>“done”.',section:'The demonstration',note:'This is an explanatory simulation, not a connected Copilot run. Replace or follow it with your live MAF demo. Use the failure switch to show why feedback and stopping conditions matter.'},
{tag:'11 / FROM THE FIELD',title:'What did it<br><em>actually cost?</em>',desc:'The useful review is about where you still intervened: context, tool access, recovery, verification and handover.',scene:'field',label:'observations to collect in Agents Window',annotation:'Bring receipts.',section:'Practical findings',note:'Draft slot for your real experience. Add one success, one failure and the concrete intervention required. Distinguish UI limitations from harness behavior. These are review prompts, not claimed findings.'},
{tag:'12 / WHERE TO USE IT',title:'Start with a task<br>you can <em>check.</em>',desc:'Good experiments have a clear boundary, accessible tools and visible evidence of completion.',scene:'ideas',label:'choose an experiment',annotation:'Small scope.<br>Strong feedback.',section:'Next experiments',note:'These are candidate experiments, not implemented integrations. Keep the first experiment small enough that you know what success means.'},
{tag:'13 / THE FACTORY QUESTION',title:'Who will build<br><em>the factory?</em>',desc:'We can assemble workflows ourselves. Which parts will become products — and where will our own design still matter?',scene:'factory',label:'custom craft → reusable product → utility?',annotation:'A hypothesis,<br>not a forecast.',section:'What comes next',note:'A simplified evolution sketch, not a formal Wardley map. Ask what differentiates your business. Avoid predicting a date for universal software factories.'},
{tag:'14 / YOUR NEXT STEP',title:'Delegate more.<br><em>Own the outcome.</em>',desc:'Pick one bounded task. Define “done”. Give the agent tools. Close the feedback loop. Then measure what you still had to do.',scene:'stairs',label:'the next step is an experiment',annotation:'What will you<br>delegate next?',section:'The invitation',note:'Return to the opening staircase. The take-away is a controlled experiment, not “build a multi-agent system everywhere”.'}
